<h2 class="bde-heading-390-178 bde-heading">
How Much Are the Hornets Paying in Total Salary?
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-211 bde-shortcode"><p>Charlotte Hornets salaries for 2026–27 add up to a cap payroll of <strong>$170.73 million</strong>, putting the club <strong>$5.77 million</strong> above the <strong>$164.96 million</strong> salary cap. That figure includes <strong>$9.04 million</strong> in dead cap for Rob Dillingham and Ryan Nembhard, both waived in September, on top of a standard roster of <strong>$161.69 million</strong>.</p>
<p>Coby White tops the payroll at <strong>$26.81 million</strong>, ahead of Naz Reid on <strong>$23.28 million</strong> and Grayson Allen on <strong>$18.13 million</strong>, together about <strong>42.2%</strong> of the roster total. Their tax salary is <strong>$29.70 million</strong> below the <strong>$200.43 million</strong> luxury-tax line and apron salary sits <strong>$37.78 million</strong> under the <strong>$209.02 million</strong> first apron.</p>
</div><div class="bde-shortcode-390-179 bde-shortcode"><div class="nst nst-component-cap" style="--nst-accent:#1D1160"><p>The Charlotte Hornets standard roster accounts for $161.69M. Add $9.04M in dead cap to reach $170.73M before cap holds. Holds are separate accounting charges, not signed salary payments.</p><div class="nst-threshold-grid"><div><h3>Salary Cap</h3><strong>$164.96M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:100%"></i></div><p>$5.77M above<br><small>Using cap payroll before holds</small></p></div><div><h3>Luxury-Tax Line</h3><strong>$200.43M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:85.183761749855%"></i></div><p>$29.70M below<br><small>Using tax salary</small></p></div><div><h3>First Apron</h3><strong>$209.02M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:81.923359567495%"></i></div><p>$37.78M below<br><small>Using apron salary</small></p></div><div><h3>Second Apron</h3><strong>$221.69M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:77.240831626715%"></i></div><p>$50.45M below<br><small>Using apron salary</small></p></div></div><div class="nst-tax-panel"><h3>Estimated Luxury-Tax Bill</h3><div class="nst-tax-summary"><strong>$0.00M</strong><span>Standard tax rates</span></div><p>The recorded tax salary is below the tax line, so this snapshot produces no luxury-tax bill. This is an estimate, not the final season-end assessment.</p><div class="nst-tax-history"><span><b>2022–23</b>Below tax</span><span><b>2023–24</b>Below tax</span><span><b>2024–25</b>Below tax</span><span><b>2025–26</b>Below tax</span></div><p class="nst-note">Three or more taxpayer seasons in the previous four trigger repeater rates. Status reviewed 2026-09-16.</p></div><h3>What Does Going Over the Tax Line Mean?</h3><p>The luxury tax is an additional team expense. It rises in bands as tax salary moves above the threshold; teams with a recent history of paying tax can face higher repeater rates. The final bill depends on the season-end calculation.</p><h3>Why the Aprons Matter</h3><p>Aprons affect how a team can build its roster, including certain exceptions and trades. Being below an apron does not automatically create cap space, and a payroll figure alone does not establish whether a hard cap has been triggered.</p><p class="nst-note">Cap holds: $4.63M. Threshold comparisons describe the recorded salary totals; they do not establish available signing room.</p></div></div>
</section><section id="team-eras" class="bde-fancy-container-390-172 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<h2 class="bde-heading-390-182 bde-heading">
Charlotte Hornets’ History and Payroll Eras
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-183 bde-shortcode"><div class="nst nst-component-eras" style="--nst-accent:#1D1160"><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>1988–2004 · Two Charlotte Beginnings</summary><p>Charlotte’s original Hornets debuted in 1988. NBA basketball returned with the Bobcats in 2004.</p></details><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>2014 · The Hornets Name Returns</summary><p>Charlotte restored the Hornets name for the 2014–15 season.</p></details><h3>The Payroll Over Time</h3><p>Across the four completed seasons shown, cap payroll ranged from $124.54M to $183.81M. The current roster puts 42.19% of active cap payroll into its three largest contracts.</p></div></div>
</section><section id="team-contracts" class="bde-fancy-container-390-173 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<h2 class="bde-heading-390-184 bde-heading">
What Do the Hornets Owe in Future Salary?
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-212 bde-shortcode"><p>What the Hornets owe in future salary is led by Brandon Miller, whose five-year extension, worth a reported <strong>$209 million</strong>, runs to <strong>2031-32</strong>. Naz Reid’s <strong>$125.00 million</strong> agreement runs to <strong>2029–30</strong>, Coby White is signed through 2028–29 at <strong>$74.00 million</strong> and Grayson Allen through 2027–28 at <strong>$70.00 million</strong>.</p>
<p>Two contracts end after this season, Grant Williams and Moussa Diabaté, so most of the current payroll still carries forward. Hannes Steinbach and Christian Anderson are also on the books to 2029–30 on rookie money.</p>
<p>For each player’s 2026–27 figure, see the full list of Charlotte Hornets salaries in the <a href="#team-roster">roster table</a>.</p>
